INFO:agent.loop:\u26a1 Executing tool: 'knowledge_agent' with arguments: {"source_title": "Conti public attribution, leaks, and DOJ case page", "source_url": "https://rewardsforjustice.net/rewards/conti/; https://www.cisecurity.org/insights/blog/the-conti-leaks-a-case-of-cybercrimes-commercialization; https://www.rapid7.com/blog/post/2022/03/01/conti-ransomware-group-internal-chats-leaked-over-russia-ukraine-conflict/; https://www.justice.gov/usao-mdtn/conti-ransomware-2", "text": "The benchmark sources describe Conti as also known as Wizard Spider and as a Russian government-linked ransomware-as-a-service group targeting U.S. and Western critical infrastructure. Rewards for Justice says aliases Target, Reshaev, Professor, Tramp, and Dandis are believed linked to Conti, and lists associated applications Conti ransomware-as-a-service, TrickBot, IcedID, Cobalt Strike, and ZLoader; it reports first detection in 2019, more than 1,000 operations, and more than 400 worldwide victims, over 290 in the U.S. CIS describes RaaS as developers leasing malware to affiliates who conduct intrusions and share profits, often 70-30; it says Conti used double extortion, had ties notably to TrickBot, and that ContiLeaks published internal chats after Conti's pro-Russia statement following the February 24, 2022 invasion. CIS reports leak-based descriptions of business units and roles: Coders, Testers, Administrators, Reverse Engineers, Penetration Testers/Hackers, with spamming contracted externally; each unit had budgets, and an insider said more than 60 staff in July 2021. CIS also reports spending on Cobalt Strike, security/antivirus testing, and Windows 11 reverse engineering. Rapid7 reports 14MB of chat logs in 393 JSON files, some encrypted and therefore incomplete; leaked communications included tools, exploits, design processes, credentials, alleged infrastructure access, and an alleged developer identity. Rapid7 assessed the scale suggested a senior leaker or coalition of disgruntled affiliates, and described recruitment interviews, coding tests, salary negotiations, product development, a large Bitcoin address, alleged FSB funding involvement, endpoint-security evasion testing, phishing, and a leaked control panel. Rapid7 assessed Conti remained active and significant after infrastructure takedowns and observed resumed normal operations by March 9, 2022. DOJ's Middle District of Tennessee page lists cases 3:23-CR-00092 (Maksim Galochkin, Maksim Rudenskiy, Mikhail Tsarev, Andrey Zhuykov) and 3:23-CR-00088 (Oleksii Lytvynenko). It states Conti affected more than 900 victims and says the defendants were charged in connection with a Conti ransomware conspiracy; Lytvynenko later pleaded guilty to Count 2 conspiracy to commit wire fraud, with sentencing scheduled for September 10, 2026. The page explicitly says defendants are presumed innocent until proven guilty beyond a reasonable doubt."}
